    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "563c9137_9262a174",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/include/asm/android_erratum_pgtable.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-11T12:25:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "do you really want to crash the system?  If this can happen, why not detect and recover and return an error?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 48,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 8
      },
      "revId": "c7b4a2ce575f34ac3c6c443dd7ac638a96044e5a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6ad8a37e_2ebd9a6f",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/include/asm/android_erratum_pgtable.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 1572468
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-11T13:57:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is a `WARN_ON` rather than a `BUG_ON`, so it shouldn\u0027t crash the system unless `panic_on_warn` is set.\n\nSince this is buried inside `set_pmd_at()`, there isn\u0027t a way to propagate an error and I can\u0027t think what the mm layer would do if this failed anyway -- the failure indicates that we\u0027re trying to map a non-cacheable buffer using huge mappings, which is not something that the workaround handles (see the changes to `asm/vmalloc.h` to prevent this). If driver code does somehow attempt this anyway, then the workaround simply won\u0027t kick in and the `WARN` offers a helpful developer diagnostic if DMA corruption later occurs.",
      "parentUuid": "563c9137_9262a174",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 48,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 8
      },
      "revId": "c7b4a2ce575f34ac3c6c443dd7ac638a96044e5a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
